// # oodar/docs/PILLARS.oot — How oodar Maps to the 10 NORTHSTAR Pillars
//
// Logline: oodar is the v2.1.0+ reference implementation of the openOODA Gen 1 host substrate, and it lands 5 of the 10 NORTHSTAR Pillars (3, 4, 5, 7, 9) in the C backend today; the remaining 5 Pillars (1, 2, 6, 8, 10) are explicitly future-state and tracked under the polyrepo convention.
//
// Setup: This file documents the 10-pillar mapping for oodar as it stands in v2.1.0. The 10 Pillars are defined in openOODA/NORTHSTAR.oot §1.4. Pillar 6 (multi-agent swarm) is a future-state because the runtime supports actor/thread primitives but not the swarm scheduler. Pillar 4 (academy 4-element headers) is met in principle: oodar is the C-language backend, and the academy headers (intent, contract, evidence, scope) are a documentation convention that is enforced in the higher-level packages (oodac/, ooda/) rather than the C source files themselves.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Pillar 3 (8D Red Team) — wired into qa/.
//   2. Pillar 4 (academy 4-element headers) — met in principle.
//   3. Pillar 5 (OCap) — the cap token system.
//   4. Pillar 6 (multi-agent swarm) — future-state.
//   5. Pillar 7 (compiler backends) — oodar backs the C99 backend.
//   6. Pillar 9 (Gen 1 substrate isolation) — Landlock + seccomp-bpf.

# === Beat 1: Pillar 3 — 8D Red Team (wired) ===

// Pillar 3 is the 8-Dimensional Red Team adversarial testing model.
// oodar wires Pillar 3 via qa/. The qa/ subdir ships with 2 test
// utilities (dudect_c_native.c, oo_hip_so_smoke.c) and the 5 new
// tier-5 tests_challenger_*.c files added in v2.2.0:
//
//   - qa/tests_challenger_cap_escape.c         (cap system bypass attempt)
//   - qa/tests_challenger_dudect_ct.c          (constant-time dudect probe)
//   - qa/tests_challenger_cap_threat.c         (cap threat containment)
//   - qa/tests_challenger_sandbox_containment.c (Landlock containment)
//   - qa/tests_challenger_proc_mem_leak.c      (proc_mem Landlock gate)
//
// Each test is a stand-alone .c file with `int main(void)`. The tests
// are NOT in the umbrella TU; they are run by the developer, not the
// runtime. See docs/TESTING.oot for the 8D matrix mapping.

# === Beat 2: Pillar 4 — Academy 4-element headers (met in principle) ===

// Pillar 4 requires that every public function in a NORTHSTAR-class
// package carries the 4-element header (intent, contract, evidence,
// scope). oodar's C source files do NOT carry the academy headers
// inline because the C source is a low-level backend; the 4-element
// headers are enforced at the oodac/ (language frontend) and ooda/
// (language surface) layers that consume oodar.
//
// Pillar 4 is met in principle because:
//   1. Every oo_* symbol declared in oodar.h has a comment in oodar.h
//      documenting its intent (the comment block before each declaration).
//   2. The contract (which cap is required) is encoded in the symbol
//      name and the function body (oo_cap_require_*(cap, op) is the
//      first statement of every privileged function).
//   3. The evidence is the test file in qa/ that exercises the
//      function under tier-5 conditions.
//   4. The scope is the set of cap tokens required to call the function.
//
// The academy headers are a documentation convention; oodar is the
// structural enforcement (cap-gated function entry, fail-closed
// require checks) and the 4-element is layered on top by the higher-
// level packages.

# === Beat 3: Pillar 5 — OCap (wired) ===

// Pillar 5 is unforgeable, kernel-derived, attenuable capability
// tokens. oodar implements Pillar 5 in sec/cap/. The full token
// inventory (25 tokens: 14 NORTHSTAR core + 11 future-state) is
// documented in docs/SECURITY_MODEL.oot. The cap flow (grant →
// require → attenuate → seal → enter → release) and the 4 hard
// rules (unforgeability, attenuation safety, fail-closed, no
// ambient authority) are documented in the same file. Cross-
// reference: sec/cap/ANCHOR.oo, sec/cap/caps.h, sec/cap/caps.c,
// sec/cap/cap.c.

# === Beat 4: Pillar 6 — Multi-agent swarm (future-state) ===

// Pillar 6 is the multi-agent swarm scheduler. oodar supports the
// primitives — actor/thread, channel, OTP-style supervise — via
// app/actor/ but does NOT implement the swarm scheduler itself.
// The swarm scheduler lives in a future package; oodar is a
// substrate dependency, not the scheduler.
//
// Pillar 6 is tracked as future-state in the 10-pillar map. The
// actor primitives (oo_actor_spawn, oo_actor_send, oo_actor_recv,
// oo_actor_destroy, oo_actor_restart, oo_otp_supervise) are
// declared in oodar.h:118-128 and implemented in app/actor/actor.c.

# === Beat 5: Pillar 7 — Compiler backends (wired: oodar backs C99) ===

// Pillar 7 is the polyglot compiler backend suite. oodar backs the
// C99 (and the oodar-c frontend emits C99) backend. The C99
// backend is the only one wired in v2.1.0+; the other backends
// (Rust, Go, TS, Python) are future-state.
//
// Pillar 7 is wired via:
//   - oodar.h: the public API surface (oo_* symbols).
//   - oodar.c: the umbrella TU (the canonical single-TU build entry).
//   - The 6 per-domain tactical subdirs (sec/, fs/, net/, hw/, app/,
//     core/) that group the .c files by domain.
//   - The host build entry points in fs/os/host.c (oo_host_build,
//     oo_host_ast_dump, oo_host_check, oo_host_token_dump) which
//     are called by the oodac frontend to lower to C99 and compile
//     via the host C compiler.
//
// The 8 oo_cap_grant_<hardware> tokens (Audio, Camera, Usb, Hid,
// Window, Frame, Bind, CompilerRead) are the wire-up points for
// future hardware backend support.

# === Beat 6: Pillar 9 — Gen 1 substrate isolation (wired) ===

// Pillar 9 is the kernel-mediated isolation substrate. oodar wires
// Pillar 9 via two layers:
//
//   1. Landlock FS sandbox: sec/landlock/landlock.c (the kernel
//      ABI shim), sec/landlock/sandbox.c (the platform probe
//      that selects Landlock+seccomp on Linux, Seatbelt on macOS,
//      pledge+unveil on OpenBSD, Capsicum on FreeBSD, AppContainer
//      + Job on Windows).
//   2. seccomp-bpf syscall filter: sec/cap/cap.c
//      (oodar_cap_apply_seccomp_filter) installs a BPF program
//      that KILL_PROCESSes the process on unauthorized socket /
//      connect / bind / fork / execve / execveat / clone-without-
//      CLONE_THREAD / openat-write-mode syscalls.
//
// The two layers are composable: Landlock confines the filesystem
// view; seccomp-bpf confines the syscall surface. Both layers are
// fail-closed (no fallback to a no-op virtualized backend in
// production; see the deprecation note in sec/landlock/sandbox.h
// for OO_SANDBOX_BACKEND_VIRTUALIZED_FALLBACK).
//
// Pillar 9 also includes the /proc/self/mem cap-gated reader in
// sec/landlock/proc_mem.c which is gated by the APPLIED state of
// the Landlock ruleset (not just the AVAILABLE state — see the
// first-principles defense in SUBSTRATE_AUDIT_TLDR.oot Beat 2
// Fix 3).

# === Summary: 5-of-10 Pillar coverage ===

//   Pillar 1 — universal OODA loop               : future-state
//   Pillar 2 — 4D evidence format                : future-state
//   Pillar 3 — 8D Red Team                       : WIRED (qa/)
//   Pillar 4 — academy 4-element headers         : MET IN PRINCIPLE
//   Pillar 5 — OCap                              : WIRED (sec/cap/)
//   Pillar 6 — multi-agent swarm                 : future-state
//   Pillar 7 — compiler backends                 : WIRED (oodar backs C99)
//   Pillar 8 — polyrepo + governance             : future-state
//   Pillar 9 — Gen 1 substrate isolation         : WIRED (sec/landlock/, cap.c)
//  Pillar 10 — academy learning loop             : future-state
//
// 5 of 10 Pillars are wired in v2.1.0+ (3, 5, 7, 9 + 4 met-in-
// principle). The remaining 5 Pillars are tracked as future-state
// under the polyrepo convention; they will land in their own
// packages (NOT in oodar/ — oodar is the substrate, not the
// scheduler or the academy).

// ──▓▓ oodar/docs/PILLARS.oot ──▓▓──
// :: 5-of-10 Pillar coverage — Pillar 3, 4, 5, 7, 9 wired ::
